    {
      "id": "s02",
      "title": "The Feeling Behind the Delay",
      "vo": "PEN: I have a form to fill out, and I keep finding other things to do first.\nPEN: It is only a few boxes, but one asks me to make an awkward decision.\nBUFF: That’s task aversiveness: one unpleasant piece can make the whole job feel bigger.\nBUFF: Procrastination can work like short-term mood repair. You switch tasks and feel better briefly.\nPEN: The relief is real, but the form stays open in the back of my mind.\nBUFF: The feeling right now can outweigh what matters later.\nPEN: So I can ask which exact part I keep trying to escape?\nBUFF: Yes. Write the unpleasant bit in plain words, like choose a date or answer honestly.\nPEN: Then I can do that piece first, or make its first move very small.\nBUFF: You don’t need to enjoy the task. You need a next move that feels doable.\nPEN: But what if even that first move feels too heavy to begin?\nBUFF: Then stop planning the whole workout. We’re only doing the warm-up.",
