    {
      "id": "s03",
      "title": "The First Rep Is the Hardest",
      "vo": "PEN: When I picture the whole report, I imagine hours of effort and no clear starting point.\nBUFF: That’s perceived effort. The full set looks heavy before you even touch the bar.\nBUFF: Set a two-minute warm-up: open the file, name it, and write one rough sentence.\nPEN: Two minutes sounds small enough that I don’t have to promise the whole evening.\nBUFF: Exactly. Starting reduces the distance between thinking about work and actually doing a rep.\nPEN: I keep waiting to feel motivated, as if the feeling must arrive first.\nBUFF: Often, motivation follows a little action. It doesn’t always lead the parade.\nPEN: And if I stop after two minutes, that still counts as beginning?\nBUFF: You showed up. On a packed day, your floor can be one sentence, not the full report.\nPEN: I can choose the tiny version now, before my mind negotiates the entire job.\nBUFF: Keep the promise small enough to repeat. A warm-up is still a real rep.\nPEN: But sometimes I start only when the deadline is breathing down my neck.",
