    {
      "id": "s04",
      "title": "Deadlines Make the Future Real",
      "vo": "PEN: I have three weeks for a presentation, so today it somehow feels optional.\nBUFF: That’s temporal discounting. A distant payoff often feels less urgent than a nearby one.\nPEN: Finishing later matters, but the distraction in front of me feels more vivid.\nBUFF: A deadline can make you move because it brings the consequence closer into view.\nPEN: That explains the last-minute sprint, but I don’t want panic doing all the coaching.\nBUFF: Make a checkpoint for tomorrow: choose the outline and have three sections named.\nPEN: Now there’s a visible finish line before the final deadline arrives.\nBUFF: Put that checkpoint in your calendar. Give the future a date and a small job.\nPEN: A smaller checkpoint seems kinder than leaving everything for one dramatic final push.\nBUFF: Use pressure as a signal to plan, not as your only source of movement.\nPEN: Another reason I stall is worrying that the first version won’t be good enough.",
