    {
      "id": "s05",
      "title": "The Blank Page Feels Safer",
      "vo": "PEN: I keep rewriting the first sentence because I want it to sound polished immediately.\nBUFF: Perfectionism can turn fear of a flawed result into a reason to delay making one.\nPEN: An empty page can’t be judged yet, so not starting feels strangely safer.\nBUFF: But the blank page gives you nothing to revise. That’s a lousy training partner.\nPEN: I could make a rough draft on purpose, without pretending it is the final version.\nBUFF: Give the draft a job: get the main idea down, even if the wording is clumsy.\nPEN: Then I can come back and improve it when there is something real to work with.\nBUFF: Set a short first pass. No polishing, no font hunt, no fake extra sets.\nPEN: It helps to separate making something from deciding whether it is any good.\nBUFF: Draft the rough version today. Editing is another rep, not permission to begin.\nPEN: I also notice my attention sliding away when I don’t know what to do next.",
