    {
      "id": "s06",
      "title": "Give Your Attention One Clear Job",
      "vo": "PEN: I open the report, then check messages, the calendar, and somehow the snack drawer.\nBUFF: Holding attention on something unclear takes effort. Easy things keep waving from the sidelines.\nPEN: The report says research, but that could mean a hundred different things.\nBUFF: A vague target makes it harder to steer your attention. Give your focus one clear cue.\nPEN: I can write the next action on paper: find the latest customer notes.\nBUFF: Make it visible beside the screen. When you drift, return to that single line.\nPEN: So I don’t have to scold myself or rebuild the entire plan each time?\nBUFF: Right. Notice the drift, read the cue, do the next small action. That’s the set.\nPEN: I can also remove some of the distractions before I start.\nBUFF: Put the phone out of reach and leave the notes open. Shape the room around the task.\nPEN: Still, a clear task can be hard to start if everything I need is hidden.",
