    {
      "id": "s08",
      "title": "Define What Done Looks Like",
      "vo": "PEN: My plan says work on the presentation, but I can’t tell what counts as progress.\nBUFF: That’s a vague goal. Without a finish line, the set can feel endless or already done.\nPEN: I keep switching between slides, research, and colors, without completing a clear piece.\nBUFF: Goal setting helps when the target names a result you can actually see.\nPEN: For this session, I could finish a rough opening slide and three section headings.\nBUFF: Good. Write that down. It gives your effort a boundary and your brain a cue.\nPEN: Then I can stop when that result exists, instead of chasing a vague feeling of enough.\nBUFF: Specific beats heroic. Pick a result that fits today’s actual time and energy.\nPEN: And if a task stays in my head after I stop, I can give it a place to land?\nBUFF: Yes. A written next step and a time can help close the mental tab for now.",
