    {
      "id": "s10",
      "title": "A Spotter and a Seven-Day Start",
      "vo": "PEN: I’ve told myself I’ll start Monday, but nobody knows what that means.\nBUFF: Tell one person what you’ll finish and by when. Ask them to check in that day.\nPEN: I don’t need an audience. I need a clear promise that someone can understand.\nBUFF: Here’s the recap. First, name the feeling and shrink the first rep until it’s doable.\nPEN: Second, shape your space and attention, then define the result that means done.\nBUFF: Third, make a nearby checkpoint, write down the next step, and ask a spotter to check in.\nPEN: For seven days, choose one task each day and write its smallest useful first move.\nBUFF: Do that move, even if it’s only a warm-up. Mark it down and keep the floor small.\nPEN: At the end of the week, notice which setup made starting feel less difficult.\nBUFF: Tiny Leg Theory is here for more clear, practical psychology. Subscribe if that helps.",
